<div id="2026-09-10-resolve-supplier-id-for-form-fill" class="cl-entry">
<h3>2026-09-10 — Resolve supplier_id server-side so the invoice dropdown pre-fills</h3>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>What.</strong> The invoice form-fill now resolves the supplier to a numeric ID on the server before navigating, instead of guessing the vendor name in the browser.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Why.</strong> The client matched vendors with a HARDCODED regex (<code>HostGator|PayPal|...|OpenAI|...</code>). "OpenRouter" is not in it (note: it lists <code>OpenAI</code>, a different vendor), so the name fell back to the literal string <code>'Supplier'</code>. Even on a match it only had a NAME, while the form's select is keyed by ID: <code>&lt;option value="[% s.id %]">[% s.name %]&lt;/option></code>. Result: supplier dropdown empty, and only the amount landed because line rows (<code>unit_cost_0</code>, <code>description_0</code>) are created dynamically by JS after the fill runs.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>How.</strong> Added <code>perform_resolve_supplier</code> to <code>Model::AI2::InvoiceCreate</code> and a <code>resolve_supplier</code> action in <code>Model::AI2::Actions</code> (POST /ai2/action). Replaced the hardcoded regex with a generic "Receipt from X" / "Invoice from X" extractor, and made the fill async: it calls resolve_supplier first, sets <code>fields.supplier_id</code>, then navigates. Never invents an ID — if no match, it returns candidates and proceeds without one.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Verified.</strong> All Perl modules and local-chat.js pass syntax check. Server-side match of 'OpenRouter, Inc' scores 260 (threshold 70). Not yet exercised end-to-end in the browser.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Files.</strong> <code>Comserv/lib/Comserv/Model/AI2/InvoiceCreate.pm</code>, <code>Comserv/lib/Comserv/Model/AI2/Actions.pm</code>, <code>Comserv/root/static/js/local-chat.js</code>.</p>
</div>
